use File::Copy qw{copy};
use File::Find();

# For secret getting/setting

use Provisioner::IPPool;
use Provisioner::Utils;
use Provisioner::Cookbook();

# Better stack traces
use Carp::Always;

# For the two merges below that accumulate what several recipes asked of a
# shared dependency -- not for _base against a domain, which Provisioner::Cookbook
# does with a merger of its own and the opposite precedence.
#
# The left is kept here on purpose: these build up a dependency's options one
# requester at a time, and where two of them genuinely disagree it is reconcile
# that settles it, having been shown both sides.  A second set_behavior naming
# the other precedence used to sit under this line and silently undo it, which
# is what inverted _base for everything.
Hash::Merge::set_behavior('STORAGE_PRECEDENT');

=head1 USAGE

    new_config [--ipmap=file] [--secrets=file] [--recipes=file] [--skip_ssh] $domain $domain2 ...

Builds a new configuration package for VM(s) to be provisioned by Troglodyne provisioner.

Per-domain Behavior is controlled by:

    /etc/trog-provisioner/recipes.yaml
    /etc/trog-provisioner/recipes.d/*.yaml
    /etc/trog-provisioner/ipmap.cfg

These describe your installation rather than this software, so they live outside
the checkout.  Point C<TROG_PROVISIONER_CONFIG> elsewhere to use another set, or
name one file at a time with the --recipes and --ipmap options.  See
L<Trog::Config>.

In the main recipes.yaml, most of what you will want will be in the _base section:

    _base:
        _global:
            registrar:
                type: "some_dns_provider_understood_by_dns-lexicon
                user: "AzureDiamond"
                key: "hunter2"
            libdir:
                - path/to/my/custom/libdir
        adminconfig:
            pkgs:
                - vim
                - tig
                - tmux
                - plocate
                - traceroute
            skel: "/opt/dotfiles/my_dude"
        data:
            from: "/opt/data"
            to: "/opt/domains"
        nosnap:
        nostubresolver:
            order: 0
        auditd:
        ufw:
        fail2ban:
        cron:
        letsencrypt:
        ...

This sets up some basic things which all your provisioned hosts will need.
You may specify any number of recipes here to cut down on the pasta in recipes.d/ .
But the most important things you will use are going to be in the _global section, as those are data used by multiple recipes.
Don't worry if you don't know about all of them yet, Recipes will crash and burn if you don't provide them the relevant information to run.

If you have some proprietary recipes to use, you can specify what libdir to search in the _global section of the domain's config in recipes.yaml.
Use the 'libdir' key (an array of library dir names to search).  By default we include 'lib'.

We use the libdir as 'base' for L<FindBin::libs> to search for the named directory, and search for the subdir 'lib' therein.

These directories ought to have both a lib/ and templates/ dir therein, structured basically the same as we do normally.

It's also useful to specify a list of what domains are shared on the same host via the _shared global section in recipes.yaml:

    _shared:
        shared.host.name:
            - guy.hosted.here
            - otherguy.hosted.here
        ...

In this example you would run `bin/new_config shared.host.name`,
and get out configs such that `bin/provision shared.host.name` in trog-provisioner can then use to provision all 3 domains on the same host.

_base and _shared sections in recipes.d/*.yaml are ignored.
This is to prevent hard to diagnose issues with configuration merging.

=head2 DRY RUNS

C<--dryrun> here means one thing and only one: an existing F<key.rsa> is kept
rather than replaced.  Everything else this program writes it still writes.

That is narrower than F<bin/provision>'s flag of the same name, which is the one
an operator passes, and it is narrow on purpose.  This program writes
configuration and destroys nothing, so there is little for a dry run to protect
-- except that key, whose public half is on a guest that is currently up.
Replacing the private half here would be losing the way in to a machine nobody
asked us to touch.  F<bin/provision> passes it through for exactly that.

=head2 IP POOL

To track which static IPs are available for new VMs, add an C<[ip_pool]> section
to ipmap.cfg alongside the C<[ips]> section:

    [ip_pool]
    ; Space-separated list of all IPs in your static pool
    addresses = 192.168.1.10 192.168.1.11 192.168.1.12 192.168.1.13

    ; OR expand a CIDR block (both keys may coexist; IPs are merged)
    ; cidr = 192.168.1.0/24

Then use C<bin/list_ip_pool> to see assignment status:

    bin/list_ip_pool [--ipmap=/etc/trog-provisioner/ipmap.cfg]

Output shows the full pool, which IPs are assigned to which VMs (from the C<[ips]>
section), and which IPs remain available for new allocations.

=head2 TESTS

Each recipe can define a list of tests to run after provisioning.
This is useful for the various things you need to work which aren't easily caught by a makefile failure, or that you'd like to report elsewhere.
See L<Provisioner::Recipe>'s tests() and testdeps() method for more details.

Reporter configuration as might be passed to L<prove> is accepted as a _global var (reporter) in recipe configuration.

The tests themselves are run on the remote host.

=head2 SECRETS

Rather than hardcoding various values into a recipe, you can instead store it in a keepass2 (kdbx) file.
By default we look for F</etc/trog-provisioner/secrets.kdbx>, but like ipmap you
can also pass it as an option.

You will be asked for the unlock password for the DB in the event you use this feature.

Example usage in a recipe:

    _base:
        _global:
            registrar:
                type: "some_dns_provider_understood_by_dns-lexicon
                user: "AzureDiamond"
                key: "secret:Hosting/DNS/password"

Where each path component past 'secret:' corresponds to group/key/parameter in a keepass KDBX.

=cut

my ( $cfile, $sfile, $pfile, $skip_ssh, $dryrun );

sub get_config {
    my $c = Config::Simple->new($cfile);
    die $Config::Simple::errstr unless $c;

    my $global_conf = $c->param( -block => 'global' );
    my $alias_conf  = $c->param( -block => 'aliases' );
    my $ns_conf     = $c->param( -block => 'nameservers' );
    my $pool_conf   = $c->param( -block => 'ip_pool' ) // {};

    # No [ips] here any more: addresses live in ips.db, where two runs
    # assigning at once cannot both take the same one.  They are read after the
    # domain is known to have a recipe, since touching the database at all is
    # something this should not do for a domain it is about to refuse.
    return ( $global_conf, $alias_conf, $ns_conf, $pool_conf );
}

# Basically build a pointer so we can modify the actual value, cuz the actual ref can't survive the iterator var :(

# Resolve the JSON::Pointer style path to deep hash param
# XXX Regrettably needs a touch() of eval()

sub get_recipe_config {
    return Provisioner::Cookbook->configuration($pfile);
}

sub expand_dependent_domains {
    my $d      = shift;
    my $c      = get_recipe_config();
    my $shared = $c->{_shared};
    return $d unless ref $shared eq 'HASH';

    foreach my $host ( keys(%$shared) ) {
        next unless ref $shared->{$host} eq 'ARRAY' && @{ $shared->{$host} };
        next unless grep { $_ eq $d } @{ $shared->{$host} };
        print "Expanding list of configs to generate to include $host\n";
        return ( $host, $d );
    }

    return $d;
}

=head2 %gaps = salvage_gaps()

What the last C<main> could not read off the guests it salvaged, keyed by
domain, and empty when there was nothing to report.

Not a return value, because this program exits 0 on these deliberately.  It
writes configuration and destroys nothing, so failing here would withhold the
configuration somebody needs in order to go and fix the permissions -- and it
runs from cron to take backups, where quitting over one unreadable directory
would abandon every domain after it.  F<bin/provision> is the program that
destroys the guest, so it is the one that has to refuse, and this is what it
asks.

Only the alarming ones: a path that is not on the guest at all has nothing to
salvage and never did, which is what every first build looks like.  See
C<_salvage_gap>.

=cut

our %SALVAGE_GAPS;

sub salvage_gaps { return %SALVAGE_GAPS }

sub main {
    my @args = @_;

    # This run's, not the last one's.  main() is called more than once in a
    # process by the tests, and would be by anything else that generated two
    # configurations.
    %SALVAGE_GAPS = ();

    my $credentials;
    GetOptionsFromArray(
        \@args,
        'ipmap=s'     => \$cfile,
        'secrets=s'   => \$sfile,
        'recipes=s'   => \$pfile,
        'skip_ssh'    => \$skip_ssh,
        'credentials' => \$credentials,
        'dryrun'      => \$dryrun,
    );

    # The passphrase to the secrets database, for a run with no terminal to ask
    # for it at.  Only when asked: see Trog::Credentials.
    Trog::Credentials->load() if $credentials;
    $skip_ssh //= 0;
    $cfile    //= Trog::Config->path('ipmap.cfg');
    $sfile    //= Trog::Config->path('secrets.kdbx');
    $pfile    //= Trog::Config->path('recipes.yaml');

    # Expand the domain list to also create those we depend upon.
    @args = uniq map { expand_dependent_domains($_) } @args;

    foreach my $d (@args) {
        my $res = handle_domain($d);
        return $res if $res;
    }
    return 0;
}

# The distribution's defaults, laid underneath what _global said.  _global
# reaches every recipe unvalidated, so a default applied only when the distro
# recipe is validated would reach no other recipe at all -- see
# Provisioner::DistroRecipe/global_defaults.
sub apply_global_defaults {
    my ( $distro, $global_config ) = @_;

    %$global_config = ( $distro->global_defaults, %$global_config );

    return $global_config;
}

# Settings that describe how the hypervisor should build the guest rather than
# what goes on it, and so belong in provision.conf rather than in a template.
#
# Asked of the recipe that consumes them rather than listed here.  There used to
# be a hardcoded list, and a comment block in example.test/provision.conf saying
# what each one did, and neither knew about the other -- so a knob added to one
# was a knob missing from the other.  bin/recipes vm prints the answer now.
sub hv_settings {
    return sort keys %{ Provisioner::Cookbook->properties( { Provisioner::Cookbook->spec('vm') } ) };
}

# Where does this guest go?  Whichever hypervisor already has it, else
# whichever has room, else -- with no fleet configured -- this machine.
sub choose_hypervisor {
    my ( $domain, $requirements ) = @_;

    # Somebody who said --connect has already decided; do not place around them.
    my $chosen = Trog::HV->new();
    return $chosen if $chosen->explicit;

    my $fleet = Trog::Hypervisors->load( Trog::Hypervisors->default_path() );
    return $chosen unless $fleet->configured;

    return $fleet->select_for( $domain, $requirements );
}

## no critic(CognitiveComplexity::ProhibitExcessCognitiveComplexity)
sub handle_domain {
    my ( $domain, $hv ) = @_;

    die "Must pass domain" unless $domain;

    my ( $global_conf, $alias_conf, $ns_conf, $pool_conf ) = get_config();

    my $basedir = $global_conf->{basedir};
    die "Must have basedir defined in global section of $cfile" unless $basedir;

    my $admin_user = $global_conf->{admin_user};
    die "Must set admin_user in global section of $cfile" unless $admin_user;

    my $admin_key = $global_conf->{admin_key};
    die "Must set admin_key in global section of $cfile" unless $admin_key;

    my $admin_gecos = $global_conf->{admin_gecos};
    die "Must set admin_gecos in global section of $cfile" unless $admin_gecos;

    my $admin_email = $global_conf->{admin_email};
    die "Must set admin_email in global section of $cfile" unless $admin_email;

    my $gateway = $global_conf->{gateway};
    die "Must set gateway (ipv4) in global section of $cfile" unless $gateway;

    my $resolvers = $global_conf->{resolvers};
    die "Must set resolvers in global section of $cfile" unless $resolvers;

    # Every domain is fully qualified, here and in recipes.d.  There is no tld
    # to append and no separate notion of an addon: a domain that is not under
    # your usual parent is just a domain, spelled out.
    my $fqdn = $domain;
    my ( $subdomain, $tld ) = split( quotemeta('.'), $domain, 2 );
    die "'$domain' is not a fully qualified domain name.\n" . "Domains are spelled out in full now, in ipmap.cfg and in recipes.d alike:\n" . "  new_config $domain.example.com\n"
      unless defined $tld && length $tld;

    # Start parsing the recipe configuration
    my $rconf = clone( get_recipe_config() );

    die "No recipe configuration found for '$domain'. Add a '$domain' key to your recipes.yaml (or recipes.d/) configuration.\n"
      unless exists $rconf->{$domain};

    # Only now, with the domain known to be one we have a recipe for, do the
    # things that cannot be taken back: an address out of the pool, and a prompt
    # for the password to the secret store.
    #
    # Seeded here for the same reason -- the first call builds the database out
    # of what every hypervisor is running, which is not work to do on behalf of
    # a domain that has no recipe.

    # A recipe carries a note saying where a password is, not the password.
    my %to_replace = Trog::Secrets->needed($rconf);
    my $keepass;
    if (%to_replace) {
        $keepass = Trog::Credentials->prompt( 'Enter password:', 'keepass' );
        my %secrets = Trog::Secrets->read( $sfile, $keepass, %to_replace );
        Trog::Secrets->apply( $rconf, %secrets );
        %secrets = ();
    }

    # Allow a base recipe set.  Read rather than removed: the merge below is
    # Provisioner::Cookbook's, and it wants the configuration whole.
    my $base_globals = clone( $rconf->{_base}{_global} // {} );

    # Figure out if we need to make this recipe depend on another
    my $shared_hosts = delete $rconf->{_shared} // {};

    my ($dep_dom) = expand_dependent_domains($domain);

    my $depends_on;
    $depends_on = $dep_dom if $dep_dom ne $domain;

    # There will always be some global config for a domain which ought be made available to the various modules
    my $global_config = delete $rconf->{$domain}{_global} // {};
    $global_config = { %$base_globals, %$global_config };

    # Which distribution is this guest?  Named in _global, and what decides the
    # base image, the packager and its invocations, which templates are searched
    # first, and which version of each recipe supplies the package names.
    #
    # Checked here rather than at the require twenty lines down, because a typo'd
    # `distro: unbuntu` that quietly fell back to the generic recipes would build
    # a guest with no packages on it at all.
    my $distro_name = $global_config->{distro} // 'ubuntu';
    die "There is no '$distro_name' distro recipe, which $domain asks for as its distro.\n" . 'There is: ' . join( ', ', Provisioner::Cookbook->distros() ) . "\n"
      unless any { $_ eq $distro_name } Provisioner::Cookbook->distros();

    # Which hypervisor is this going on?
    #
    # The recipe says how much memory, disk and CPU the guest wants, which is
    # everything placement needs -- so this is decided here rather than after
    # provision.conf is written, and provision.conf never has to carry it.
    $hv //= choose_hypervisor( $domain, $global_config );

    # A hypervisor that addresses its own guests leaves the pool nothing to do:
    # there is no address of ours to hand out, and taking one would reserve it
    # against a guest that will never answer at it.
    my ( $static_ip, $ip_conf ) = ( undef, {} );
    if ( !$hv->manages_addresses ) {
        Provisioner::IPPool::ensure_seeded($pool_conf);

        # One transaction, so a fan-out of provisions cannot hand two guests the
        # same address.  Idempotent, so re-provisioning keeps what this domain has.
        $static_ip = Provisioner::IPPool::assign( $domain, $pool_conf );
        $ip_conf   = Provisioner::IPPool::assignments();

        # Every domain is spelled out, so a bare label is a leftover from when there
        # was a tld to append and is worth saying so about rather than quietly
        # letting it into a zone file.
        my @unqualified = grep { index( $_, q{.} ) < 0 } sort keys %$ip_conf;
        die "These domains in " . Provisioner::IPPool::db_path() . " are not fully qualified:\n" . join( '', map { "  $_\n" } @unqualified ) . "There is no tld to append any more.  Spell them out, as recipes.d already does.\n"
          if @unqualified;
    }

    # Who the guest fetches its payload from, and where it reaches them: us, on
    # all three counts.  Nothing is copied to the hypervisor for the guest to
    # pull from; it comes to this machine for it.
    my $this_machine = Trog::Local->new();

    # The hypervisor's NAT bridge, which is one of the networks we may share
    # with the guest.  A cloud has no such bridge, and no address of its own
    # that a guest is reached over.
    my $tld_ip        = $hv->manages_addresses ? undef : $hv->virbr_ip;
    my $transfer_port = $global_conf->{transfer_port} // $this_machine->sshd_port;
    my $transfer_user = $global_conf->{transfer_user} // $this_machine->transfer_user;
    die "Could not work out the transfer user on " . $this_machine->describe . "\n" unless $transfer_user;

    # Which of our addresses is a question about how this machine is attached to
    # the networks the guest will be on, so it is asked rather than assumed --
    # and overridable, because a machine with several routes to the same place
    # is not something the kernel can pick between on our behalf.
    #
    # The guest's own address first, then the hypervisor's NAT bridge: we may
    # share either with it, and sharing the static subnet is the case a
    # workstation that is not itself a guest is in.
    # Every address of ours that reaches the guest, not only the one it fetches
    # from.  Which one we arrive on depends on the hypervisor -- a remote one is
    # administered over the guest's static address and a local one over its NAT
    # lease -- so the guest's firewall has to expect all of them.  The fetch
    # takes the first, because the rsync in the template names one address.
    my @towards = grep { defined $_ && length $_ } ( $static_ip, $tld_ip );

    my @transfer_ips = $global_conf->{transfer_ip} ? ( $global_conf->{transfer_ip} ) : ( @towards ? $this_machine->transfer_ips(@towards) : () );
    my $transfer_ip  = $transfer_ips[0];

    # Nothing to aim at is a different problem from nothing that reaches.  A
    # cloud allocates the guest's address when it creates it, so at this point
    # there is no address to ask the routing table about -- and the answer has
    # to be given rather than worked out.
    die "There is no address of $fqdn to work out which of ours it can reach.\n" . $hv->describe . " allocates that when the guest is created, so it cannot be\n" . "known here.  Name the address a guest fetches from as transfer_ip in the\n" . "[global] section of ipmap.cfg.\n"
      if !$transfer_ip && !@towards;

    die "Nothing on " . $this_machine->describe . " can be reached from $fqdn.\n" . "A guest fetches its payload from here, so it needs an address of ours it can\n" . "get to -- and none of " . join( ', ', @towards ) . " routes anywhere from this\n" . "machine.  Put it on one of those networks, or name the address as\n" . "transfer_ip in the [global] section of ipmap.cfg.\n"
      unless $transfer_ip;

    # The same fold as anything else asking what a domain is configured with,
    # over this copy rather than the file, so the secrets resolved above survive
    # it.
    # Where this domain lives, and where what gets shipped to it comes from.
    # Asked before _base goes, because that is where a fleet says it once for
    # every domain -- and asked of Cookbook, which is what knows that a
    # configuration written before the move says it under data instead.
    #
    # _global's to say, not the data recipe's: every recipe interpolates
    # install_dir, and reading it out of data's `to` made all of them depend on
    # that recipe for a path rather than for anything it does, which is what
    # kept data from being an ordinary recipe.
    $global_config->{install_dir} //= Provisioner::Cookbook->install_dir( $domain, $rconf );
    $global_config->{data_source} //= Provisioner::Cookbook->data_source( $domain, $rconf );

    $rconf->{$domain} = Provisioner::Cookbook->domain_config( $domain, $rconf );
    delete $rconf->{_base};

    # bin/new_guest writes CHANGEME wherever a recipe requires something it has
    # no default for.  A placeholder is a perfectly good string, so nothing
    # further down would object to one -- validation passes, the guest builds,
    # and the database ends up with a root password of CHANGEME.  So this is
    # where somebody looks.  After the merge, because _base may well have
    # supplied the real value.
    my @unfilled = Provisioner::Cookbook->placeholders_in( $rconf->{$domain} );
    die "Still unfilled in the configuration for $domain:\n" . join( '', map { "  $_\n" } @unfilled ) . "Each of these is a " . Provisioner::Cookbook->PLACEHOLDER . " that new_guest left for you.\n" if @unfilled;

    # Include site-specific stuff
    my $libdirs = $global_config->{libdir} // [];
    foreach my $libdir (@$libdirs) {
        print "Including vendor libs from $libdir/lib\n";
        push( @INC, "$libdir/lib" );
    }

    # The distribution's own directory first, so templates/ubuntu/nginx.tt wins
    # over templates/nginx.tt by being found first -- which is the whole
    # mechanism, and needs no code that knows about it.
    my @paths = @{ Provisioner::Cookbook->template_dirs( $distro_name, @$libdirs ) };

    # Read, not deleted.  It used to be lifted out of the configuration entirely,
    # which is what kept it from being a recipe the depsolver could see -- and so
    # from being handed anything by the recipes that depend on it.  It stays in,
    # takes part in the loop like everything else, and is only lifted back out at
    # the makefile render, where its target has a fixed slot ahead of the others.
    # Optional now.  It was required because install_dir was read out of it, so a
    # domain without one had nowhere to put anything.  That comes from _global,
    # so a guest with nothing to ship and no state to restore simply gets no
    # data target.
    my $data_config = $rconf->{$domain}{data} // {};

    # Where this domain's files live, and where what gets shipped to it comes
    # from.  _global's to say, not the data recipe's: every recipe interpolates
    # install_dir, and reading it out of data's `to` made all of them depend on
    # that recipe for a path rather than for anything it does -- which is what
    # kept data from being an ordinary recipe.  Cookbook still answers with
    # data's own fields for a configuration written before the move.
    #
    # Here rather than further down, so that everything after it -- the tests
    # data renders, what each recipe is asked to salvage -- is asked with the
    # same answer.
    die "Nothing says where $fqdn's data comes from: set data_source in _global, or from under data.\n"
      unless defined $global_config->{data_source} && length $global_config->{data_source};

    my $data_dir = "$global_config->{data_source}/$fqdn";

    my @data_users;
    my $data_users_f = "$data_dir/users.yaml";
    ## no critic (ValuesAndExpressions::ProhibitFiletest_f)
    if ( -f $data_users_f ) {
        my $data_users_yaml = YAML::XS::Load( File::Slurper::read_binary($data_users_f) );
        @data_users = @{ $data_users_yaml->{users} };
    }

    # Build a users.yaml based on ipmap, and any users.yaml found in the DATA dir.
    my $user_conf = {
        users => [
            {
                gecos         => $admin_gecos,
                name          => $admin_user,
                shell         => '/bin/bash',
                sudo          => 'ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL',
                ssh_import_id => [$admin_key],
            },
            @data_users,
        ],
    };

    my $cfg_dir = "$basedir/$fqdn";
    if ( !-d $cfg_dir ) {
        mkdir $cfg_dir or die "could not create $cfg_dir";
    }

    File::Slurper::Temp::write_binary( "$cfg_dir/users.yaml", YAML::XS::Dump($user_conf) );

    # Expose user list to all templates (e.g. ldap seed LDIF)
    $global_config->{users} = $user_conf->{users};

    my %fragments;
    my %global_fragments;

    my $root_scripts = '/root/bin';

    my %formatters = (
        tabinate => Text::Xslate::html_builder(
            sub {
                my $input = shift;
                $input =~ s/^\s*/\t/mg;
                return $input;
            }
        ),
    );

    my $tt = Text::Xslate->new(
        {
            path     => \@paths,
            syntax   => 'TTerse',
            module   => [qw{Text::Xslate::Bridge::TT2}],
            function => \%formatters,
        }
    ) || die "Could not initialize template dir";

    # The class, not an instance: everything asked of it here is a fact about the
    # distribution rather than about this guest, and the object that renders its
    # files is built by the depsolver below like every other recipe's.  Two
    # objects for one recipe would be two chances to generate the guest's key.
    my $distro = Provisioner::Cookbook->load($distro_name);

    my %provisioner_opts = (
        distro          => $distro_name,
        target_packager => $distro->packager,
        script_loc      => $root_scripts,
        output_dir      => $cfg_dir,
        template_dirs   => \@paths,
    );

    # Every web host wants its own www and mail CNAMEs.  This used to have two
    # spellings -- bare for an addon, prefixed otherwise -- because one of them
    # had a tld appended afterwards and the other did not.  Both arrived at
    # www.<fqdn>; with everything spelled out there is only the one way to say
    # it.  Note this is a different variable from the $subdomain above: it is
    # every domain in the map, not the one we are building.
    foreach my $each_domain ( keys(%$ip_conf) ) {
        $alias_conf->{$each_domain} = Provisioner::Utils::coerce_arrayref( $alias_conf->{$each_domain} );
        push( @{ $alias_conf->{$each_domain} }, map { "$_.$each_domain" } qw{www mail} );
        $alias_conf->{$each_domain} = [ uniq @{ $alias_conf->{$each_domain} } ];
    }

    # The guest rsyncs its payload before it has working DNS, so this has to be
    # an address and not a name.
    $global_config->{transfer_ip}  = $transfer_ip;
    $global_config->{transfer_ips} = \@transfer_ips;
    $global_config->{tld}          = $tld;
    $global_config->{admin_user}   = $admin_user;
    $global_config->{admin_email}  = $admin_email;

    # The other two halves of who administers a guest and where it sits, out of
    # the same [global] block as the two above.  They were the only members of
    # it a recipe could not see, which read as an oversight rather than a
    # decision -- Provisioner::Recipe::trogrunner is what noticed, needing to
    # write an ipmap.cfg of its own and having no way to ask what this one says.
    $global_config->{admin_key}     = $admin_key;
    $global_config->{gateway}       = $gateway;
    $global_config->{domain}        = $fqdn;
    $global_config->{ipmap}         = $ip_conf;
    $global_config->{subdomain}     = $domain;
    $global_config->{aliases}       = $alias_conf;
    $global_config->{nameservers}   = $ns_conf;
    $global_config->{main_ip}       = $static_ip;
    $global_config->{tld_ip}        = $tld_ip;
    $global_config->{transfer_user} = $transfer_user;
    $global_config->{transfer_port} = $transfer_port;

    apply_global_defaults( $distro, $global_config );

    # Worked out once, here, rather than by each recipe -- see
    # Provisioner::DistroRecipe/cache_address.  And resolvers, which were the
    # distro recipe's alone, for every recipe: one running a resolver of its
    # own -- nginx, in the fetch cache -- needs to know where to send it.
    $global_config->{cache_ip}  = $distro->cache_address(%$global_config);
    $global_config->{resolvers} = Provisioner::Utils::coerce_arrayref($resolvers);

    # Where every recipe's tests are written, data's included: it is a module
    # now, so the loop below renders them out of the configuration it ended up
    # with rather than out of what it started with.
    mkdir "$cfg_dir/t";

    $global_config->{script_dir} = $root_scripts;

    # If we use a local dns provider, we might be doing NS delegation to this box.
    # As such we have to teach anything relying on DNS changes to use it.
    $global_config->{local_dns_access_token} = get_dns_provider_token( $rconf->{$domain} );

    my ( @pkgs, @generated );

    # Regrettably, we may be running this on an existing system so we gotta install pkgs anyways.
    push( @pkgs, $distro->deps() );

    $global_config->{packager_invocation}        = $distro->packager_invocation;
    $global_config->{packager_up_invocation}     = $distro->packager_up_invocation;
    $global_config->{packager_remove_invocation} = $distro->packager_remove_invocation;

    # Figure out whether the remote host is actually up.
    #
    # Over sftp because opening the connection is the probe, and that is all this
    # handle is for now.  It used to answer _salvage_gap's question as well --
    # whether a directory that salvaged nothing was missing or merely unreadable,
    # which was one status code here -- but the fetch reads what the service owns
    # now, so the question became whether the path holds anything, which sftp
    # cannot answer without the privileges the fetch already uses.  Nothing is
    # fetched over this.  That is _salvage_guest's job.
    my ( $ssh, $target_is_up );

    # Only if we know of an address to try.  A guest whose hypervisor allocates
    # its own has none until it has been built, and there is nothing to salvage
    # off a guest that does not exist yet.
    ( $ssh, $target_is_up ) = _get_sftp( $static_ip, $admin_user, $cfg_dir )
      if !$skip_ssh && defined $static_ip && length $static_ip;

    # Build a makefile based on our configured recipes.
    #
    # The distro recipe goes in the list so that it is depsolved like anything
    # else -- it is what pulls the vm recipe in, and both of them are configured
    # by the same merge every other recipe is.  Neither ends up a module; they
    # are partitioned back out below.
    my @modules = ( $distro_name, sort { ( $rconf->{$domain}{$a}{order} // 'ZZZ' ) cmp ( $rconf->{$domain}{$b}{order} // 'ZZZ' ) } sort keys( %{ $rconf->{$domain} } ) );

    my %mf_vars;
    my @testdeps;
    my @empty_salvage;
    my @conflicts;
    my @modules_ordered;
    my $depmod_conf = {};

    #Memoize
    my %builders;

    # Each recipe's configuration as the domain wrote it, taken on its first
    # visit.  A recipe other recipes depend on is visited once for each of
    # them, and what they handed it has to be merged into what the domain
    # wrote each time -- merged into the last visit's result instead, a list
    # they handed it came out once per visit.  perl's cpan_deps did, three
    # times over, on a guest with tcms, tpsgi and trogrunner.
    my %as_written;

    # First, run thru the modules and depsolve.
    for ( my $i = 0; $i < scalar(@modules); $i++ ) {
        my $module  = $modules[$i];
        my $builder = $builders{$module} //= Provisioner::Cookbook->load( $module, distro => $distro_name )->new(%provisioner_opts);

        my $pconf = $rconf->{$domain}{$module} // {};

        # Check and see if this particular provisioner requires another recipe to work.
        #
        # The base's own answer first, then the recipe's.  What the base decides
        # every recipe owes -- ufw its rate limits, data its restores -- is not
        # something an override should be able to drop by forgetting to chain to
        # SUPER, and six of them do exactly that.  Asking it here means a recipe
        # only has to say what is true of itself.
        my %dep_recipes = (
            Provisioner::Recipe::required_recipes( $builder, %$global_config, %$pconf ),
            $builder->required_recipes( %$global_config, %$pconf ),
        );
        foreach my $required ( keys(%dep_recipes) ) {
            my $manual_args = delete $pconf->{$required} // {};

            # See about autocomputing the options if possible.
            my %depargs  = $dep_recipes{$required}->( %$global_config, %$pconf ) if ref $dep_recipes{$required} eq 'CODE';
            my %cur_args = %$manual_args ? ( $required => $manual_args ) : ( $required => \%depargs );

            # The big problem here is simple. Dep recipes can be rendered only once, but sometimes accomodate data from multiple requestors.
            # As such we have to make sure they get rendered last, and progressively add to their data depth-first.
            push( @modules, $required ) unless any { $required eq $_ };
            $depmod_conf = Hash::Merge::merge( $depmod_conf, \%cur_args );

            # Hash::Merge picks a side where two dependants disagree.  The recipe
            # being depended on is the only thing that knows whether either side
            # is right, so it gets asked -- and dies if it does not know.
            Provisioner::Cookbook->load( $required, distro => $distro_name )->reconcile( $depmod_conf->{$required}, $cur_args{$required} );
        }

        # Merge the configuration provided by all things depending on this.
        $as_written{$module} //= clone($pconf);
        if ( $depmod_conf->{$module} ) {
            $rconf->{$domain}{$module} = Hash::Merge::merge( $depmod_conf->{$module}, $as_written{$module} );

            # Same on this side of it: what an operator wrote for this recipe is
            # held against what the recipes depending on it asked for.
            $builder->reconcile( $rconf->{$domain}{$module}, $_ ) for ( $depmod_conf->{$module}, $as_written{$module} );
        }
    }

    # Deps should be processed after the last thing that dragged them in, and not before.
    # This is sometimes the case when we have multiple recipes depending on the same thing!
    @modules = Provisioner::Utils::lastuniq(@modules);

    # And out come the two that direct the build rather than running in it --
    # the distro recipe and the vm recipe it depends on.  Dropped here rather
    # than never added, because they had to go through the depsolve above to be
    # configured and to pull each other in.
    #
    # They are left out of @modules for more than the makefile, which skips a
    # recipe with no fragment anyway: `modules` is handed to every template and
    # every recipe as the list of what is on this guest, and neither of these is.
    @modules = grep { $builders{$_}->is_module } @modules;

    my %guest_secrets;

    # Actually process the modules now that we have solved deps.
    foreach my $module (@modules) {
        my $builder = $builders{$module};
        my $pconf   = $rconf->{$domain}{$module};

        # Aliases are fully qualified in ipmap.cfg as well; nothing is appended.
        my $aliases      = $alias_conf->{$domain} // [];
        my $full_aliases = [@$aliases];
        if ( $builder->has_global_template() ) {
            $global_fragments{$module} = $builder->render_global(
                %$global_config,
                %$pconf,
                full_aliases => $full_aliases,
                modules      => \@modules,
            );
            push( @modules_ordered, "/etc/provisioner/state/global_$module" );
        }
        if ( $builder->has_template() ) {
            $fragments{$module} = $builder->render(
                %$global_config,
                %$pconf,
                full_aliases => $full_aliases,
                modules      => \@modules,
            );
            push( @modules_ordered, "/etc/provisioner/state/$fqdn/$module" );
        }
        push( @pkgs,      ( $builder->deps(%$pconf) ) );
        push( @conflicts, ( $builder->dep_conflicts(%$pconf) ) );

        # Grab the relevant makefile vars for the target
        my %t_vars = $builder->makefile_vars();
        %mf_vars = ( %mf_vars, %t_vars ) if %t_vars;

        # Files the guest has to have that must not travel in the payload: the
        # store holds them, bin/provision puts them there.  Collected here and
        # resolved in one go below, so a run wants the store password once.
        %guest_secrets = ( %guest_secrets, $builder->guest_secrets( $global_config->{install_dir}, $fqdn ) );

        # Make dirs in the DATADIR needed by ssh
        foreach my $d ( $builder->datadirs() ) {
            make_path("$data_dir/$d") unless -d "$data_dir/$d";
        }

        # Schlep over stuff from existing deployments
        if ($target_is_up) {
            print "Backing up $module files from $fqdn\n";
            my %tg   = $builder->remote_files( $global_config->{install_dir}, $fqdn );
            my @skip = $builder->remote_skip();

            # Asked before any of it comes down, so what does is what the guest
            # looks like now rather than whenever its cron last ran.
            refresh_salvage( $static_ip, $admin_user, $cfg_dir, $module, [ $builder->remote_prepare( $global_config->{install_dir}, $fqdn ) ], [ sort keys %tg ] );
            foreach my $to_fetch ( sort keys(%tg) ) {
                print "Fetching $to_fetch into $data_dir/$tg{$to_fetch}\n";

                # Not checked, on purpose.  A salvage that came away with
                # nothing is what _salvage_gap below is for, and it says
                # something more useful about why than a return value does; one
                # that came away with some of it has still left the domain
                # better off than abandoning the rest would.
                _salvage_guest( $static_ip, $admin_user, $cfg_dir )->get_dir(
                    $to_fetch,
                    "$data_dir/$tg{$to_fetch}",

                    # These are directories, and a recipe can have something in
                    # one that is meant to stay on the machine it was made on --
                    # see remote_skip in Provisioner::Recipe.  Left behind rather
                    # than fetched and deleted: this runs against a live guest,
                    # and the guest still wants it.
                    exclude => \@skip,

                    # What the sftp fetch called newer_only.  A guest's copy that
                    # is older than ours stays where it is.
                    update => 1,

                    # As root on the guest.  What a recipe salvages is a
                    # service's own state directory, which the service owns and
                    # nobody else can open -- redis is 0750 redis:redis, slapd.d
                    # is 0700 openldap, a PKI is 0600 root.  Unprivileged, this
                    # made the local directories, copied nothing into them and
                    # reported success, which looks exactly like a guest that has
                    # never run the service.
                    sudo => 1,
                );

                my $gap = _salvage_gap(
                    guest       => _salvage_guest( $static_ip, $admin_user, $cfg_dir ),
                    recipe      => $module,
                    host        => $fqdn,
                    user        => $admin_user,
                    remote      => $to_fetch,
                    destination => "$data_dir/$tg{$to_fetch}",
                );
                next unless $gap;

                # warn rather than printed: the rest of a run is a wall of
                # progress on STDOUT, and the line saying a service's state did
                # not come down has to survive being scrolled past.  warn also
                # goes through the warn handler, so a caller that wants these
                # somewhere other than the terminal can have them.
                warn $gap->{message} . "\n";
                push( @empty_salvage, $gap ) if $gap->{alarming};
            }
        }

        # Now build the various templates for the recipes.  These go in the
        # payload, which is what separates them from the director's files below.
        push( @generated, $builder->generate_files( $cfg_dir, %$global_config, %$pconf, full_aliases => $full_aliases, modules => \@modules ) );

        # Write tests if any exist
        my @tests = $builder->tests();
        foreach my $test (@tests) {
            my $tname = $test;
            $tname =~ s/tt$/t/;
            my $rendered = $builder->render_file( "tests/$test", %$global_config, %$pconf, full_aliases => $full_aliases, modules => \@modules );
            print "Writing $test as $tname\n";
            File::Slurper::Temp::write_binary( "$cfg_dir/t/$tname", $rendered );
        }

        # Build test deps
        push( @testdeps, ( $builder->testdeps(@modules) ) );
    }
    @testdeps = uniq @testdeps;
    push( @pkgs, 'cpanminus' ) if @testdeps;

    @pkgs      = sort { $a cmp $b } uniq @pkgs;
    @conflicts = uniq @conflicts;

    # Eject any conflicting deps identified by recipes.
    @pkgs = grep {
        my $pkg = $_;
        !any { $_ eq $pkg } @conflicts;
    } @pkgs if @conflicts;

    my $extra_packages = join( ', ', @pkgs );

    # Out of the module machinery, and only here.  data took part in the loop
    # like any other recipe -- which is what let the recipes hand it their
    # restores -- but its target is not one of the ordered ones: makefile.tt
    # places it by hand, ahead of everything, because a recipe finding its state
    # already restored is the entire point.
    #
    # Both lines are needed.  The first supplies the data_fragment the template
    # expects, and leaving it in %fragments would emit a second state/data:
    # target that make would override the first with.  The second keeps it off
    # all:'s prerequisite list, where it would run last.
    #
    # Compared exactly rather than matched, so a domain called 'data' does not
    # lose its own target.
    my $data_fragment = delete $fragments{data};
    @modules_ordered = grep { $_ ne "/etc/provisioner/state/$fqdn/data" } @modules_ordered;

    # ufw comes out for the opposite reason: it has to run after every other
    # recipe rather than before them, and templates/makefile.tt says why.
    # Ordering it there rather than here is what stops the alphabet deciding it.
    my $ufw_fragment = delete $fragments{ufw};
    @modules_ordered = grep { $_ ne "/etc/provisioner/state/$fqdn/ufw" } @modules_ordered;

    # The hosts this guest's recipes download from, pointed at the fetch cache
    # while it provisions, and the authority the cache's certificate is signed
    # by, for the guest to trust meanwhile: see Provisioner::Recipe/fetch_hosts
    # and scripts/fetch_via_cache.  Only the certificate travels; its key stays
    # on this machine.
    # Asked with the module's own configuration, so a recipe whose upstream is
    # configured -- a repo_url or an api_url pointed somewhere other than the
    # default -- names the host it will actually reach.  Cookbook::fetch_hosts,
    # which the cache builds its certificate from, is asked of the class and
    # cannot see any of this: a cache serving a configured upstream needs that
    # host in its own upstreams.
    my @fetch_hosts =
      $global_config->{cache_ip}
      ? uniq( sort map { $builders{$_}->fetch_hosts( %$global_config, %{ $rconf->{$domain}{$_} // {} } ) } @modules )
      : ();
    if (@fetch_hosts) {
        copy( Provisioner::Cookbook->load('fetchcache')->authority()->{cert}, "$cfg_dir/fetchcache-ca.crt" )
          or die "Could not give $fqdn the fetch cache's authority: $!\n";

        # Into the tarball with what the recipes generated; nothing else in
        # $cfg_dir reaches the guest.
        push( @generated, 'fetchcache-ca.crt' );
    }

    my $makefile = $tt->render(
        'makefile.tt',
        {
            %$global_config,
            data_fragment    => $data_fragment,
            ufw_fragment     => $ufw_fragment,
            fragments        => \%fragments,
            global_fragments => \%global_fragments,
            modules_ordered  => \@modules_ordered,

            # The ssl target names each of these in the certificate it makes.
            full_aliases     => $alias_conf->{$fqdn} // [],
            vars             => \%mf_vars,
            packages         => \@pkgs,
            state_dir        => "/etc/provisioner/state/$fqdn",
            global_state_dir => "/etc/provisioner/state",
            testdeps         => \@testdeps,
            fetch_hosts      => @fetch_hosts ? \@fetch_hosts : undef,
        }
    );

    print "Writing Makefile to $cfg_dir\n";
    File::Slurper::Temp::write_binary( "$cfg_dir/Makefile", $makefile );

    write_guest_secrets( $cfg_dir, $fqdn, \%guest_secrets, $sfile, $keepass );

    my $r_text = join( ', ', @$resolvers );

    # The files a guest first boots from: its network configuration, its
    # cloud-init, the setup script that fetches the payload, and where it ships
    # its logs.  Ordinary template_files of the distro recipe, rendered the way
    # every other recipe's generated files are.
    #
    # After the loop rather than in it, because the user-data lists the packages
    # passed in below, and @pkgs is not final until every recipe's deps have
    # been collected and the conflicts ejected.
    #
    # Being last inside the loop would answer that too, and is how a dependency
    # normally gets its turn -- lastuniq puts a recipe after the last thing that
    # dragged it in.  It is the worse option because it only holds while every
    # recipe on the guest drags this one in: it would mean injecting the
    # dependency into every recipe in the distribution's namespace, and
    # trusting that nothing outside it -- a vendor recipe, some later
    # required_recipes -- ever sorts after them.  An explicit pass needs no such
    # trust.
    #
    # It also keeps these out of the payload, which they have to be: user-data
    # carries the private half of the guest's key, and setup.sh reaches the
    # guest inside cloud-init rather than in the tarball it is the script for
    # fetching.  In the loop that would be a special case in the loop.
    #
    # The distro recipe's, and only its.  The other director is the vm recipe,
    # whose one file names a storage volume, a base image and a seed ISO by
    # paths that do not exist until a hypervisor has made them -- so that one is
    # generated by bin/provision, which is the program that has one in hand.
    print "Writing what $fqdn first boots from, out of the $distro_name recipe\n";
    $builders{$distro_name}->generate_files(
        $cfg_dir,
        %$global_config,
        %{ $rconf->{$domain}{$distro_name} // {} },
        packages      => \@pkgs,
        ips           => [ grep { defined } $static_ip ],
        gateway       => $gateway,
        contact_email => $admin_email,
        payload_dir   => $basedir,
        dryrun        => $dryrun,
        modules       => \@modules,
    );

    # Build a provision.conf

    # The image is the distribution's to name, and how big the guest is is the vm
    # recipe's -- the same schema bin/new_guest writes a domain block from and
    # `bin/recipes vm` prints.
    my $image = $distro->base_image;
    my %vm    = Provisioner::Cookbook->defaults('vm');
    my $size  = $global_config->{size}   // $vm{size};
    my $cpus  = $global_config->{cpus}   // $vm{cpus};
    my $mem   = $global_config->{memory} // $vm{memory};

    my $pconf = qq(
        distro=$distro_name
        image=$image
        size=$size
        cpus=$cpus
        memory=$mem
        packages=$extra_packages
        contact_email=$admin_email
        ips=@{[ $static_ip // q{} ]}
        admin_user=$admin_user
        gateway=$gateway
        resolvers=$r_text
        transfer_ip=$transfer_ip
        transfer_user=$transfer_user
        transfer_port=$transfer_port
        payload_dir=$basedir
    );
    $pconf .= "depends_on=$depends_on" if $depends_on;

    # The optional settings for the hypervisor side of the build, passed through
    # from _global when they are there.  None of them get a default here: what
    # this hypervisor's libvirt and qemu will take is worked out in
    # bin/provision, and a key that is absent means "whatever it decides".
    #
    # Absent rather than empty, because Config::Simple hands back an empty string
    # for a bare `key=` and an empty string is not the same as no answer.  And
    # never one of the keys written out above, which would be a duplicate.
    my %written = map { m/\A\s*(\w+)=/ ? ( $1 => 1 ) : () } split( "\n", $pconf );
    $pconf .= join( '', map { "\n$_=$global_config->{$_}" } grep { !$written{$_} && defined $global_config->{$_} && length $global_config->{$_} } hv_settings() );

    File::Slurper::Temp::write_binary( "$cfg_dir/provision.conf", $pconf );

    # Both of these live next to this script, not in whatever directory it was
    # run from.  Anchoring them to the checkout is what lets the generator run
    # from anywhere -- which it now does, since it no longer runs on the
    # hypervisor.
    my $checkout = Cwd::abs_path( File::Basename::dirname(__FILE__) . '/..' );

    # Slap in openssl.conf to make self-signed cert work
    print "Copying openssl.conf to $cfg_dir/openssl.conf\n";
    copy( "$checkout/openssl.conf", "$cfg_dir/openssl.conf" )
      or die "Could not copy openssl.conf into $cfg_dir: $!\n";

    # The guest used to rsync these off the hypervisor, which only worked while
    # the hypervisor had a checkout at the same path this one is at.  It does
    # not, so they ride along in the tarball and the Makefile just moves them
    # into place.
    my @scripts = pack_scripts( $checkout, $cfg_dir );

    # Gzip the Makefile & generated configs
    print "Tarring up Makefile and generated configuration\n";
    unlink "$cfg_dir/data.tar.gz";

    my @members = ( 'Makefile', @generated, 'openssl.conf', @scripts );
    File::Find::find(
        sub {
            my $rel = $File::Find::name;
            $rel =~ s{^\Q$cfg_dir\E/}{};
            push( @members, $rel );
        },
        "$cfg_dir/t"
    );

    my $tar = Archive::Tar->new();
    $tar->add_files( map { "$cfg_dir/$_" } @members ) or die "Could not add files to $cfg_dir/data.tar.gz: " . Archive::Tar->error();

    # add_files stores each path as given, so re-point the stored names at $cfg_dir
    foreach my $member ( $tar->get_files() ) {
        my $stored = $member->full_path();
        $stored =~ s{^\Q$cfg_dir\E/}{};
        $member->rename($stored);
    }

    $tar->write( "$cfg_dir/data.tar.gz", Archive::Tar::COMPRESS_GZIP() ) or die "Could not write $cfg_dir/data.tar.gz: " . Archive::Tar->error();

    # The data directory has to exist even when there is nothing in it.  A domain
    # that has never existed has no data of its own yet, and the guest's rsync
    # fails rather than shrugging when the source directory is not there -- which
    # fails the data target and takes the rest of the Makefile with it before any
    # other recipe has run.
    make_path($data_dir) unless -d $data_dir;

    # Nobody remembers the one line in the middle of a run this long, so the
    # empty salvages get said again where the run ends and the operator is
    # actually looking.
    #
    # Loud, and not fatal.  This program writes configuration; it destroys
    # nothing, so dying here protects none of the state it just failed to read
    # -- it only withholds the configuration somebody needs in order to go and
    # fix the permissions and try again.  It is also run from cron to take
    # backups, where quitting over one unreadable directory would abandon every
    # domain after it, including the ones that were fine.  bin/provision is what
    # rebuilds the guest and loses the data; this is the thing that tells you
    # not to run it yet.
    if (@empty_salvage) {
        warn "\n" . scalar(@empty_salvage) . " salvage(s) on $fqdn came back with nothing:\n" . join( q{}, map { "    $_->{recipe} read nothing out of $_->{remote}\n" } @empty_salvage ) . "None of that state is in $data_dir.  Rebuilding $fqdn now loses it.\n";

        # Kept for whoever is going to act on it.  Saying so on the way past is
        # the most this program can do about it; refusing to destroy the guest
        # is bin/provision's to do, and this is how it finds out.
        $SALVAGE_GAPS{$fqdn} = [@empty_salvage];
    }

    print "Done.\n";
    return 0;
}

=head2 refresh_salvage($static_ip, $admin_user, $cfg_dir, $module, $commands, $watched)

Ask the guest to bring what is about to be salvaged up to date.

A dump written nightly and fetched at noon is twelve hours out of date, and the
difference is everything that happened in between -- which is exactly what a
rebuild is trying not to lose.  A recipe says how to refresh its own state in
C<remote_prepare>; this runs it, as root, on the guest being salvaged.

A failure is usually a warning.  The guest may not have the script yet, and last
night's dump is worth more than none -- which is what dying here would leave.

B<Unless it changed something first.>  C<$watched> is what is about to come
down, and the newest mtime under each of those paths is taken before and after
every command.  A command that failed having touched none of them left last
night's dump exactly as it was, and that is the case the warning is for.  One
that failed having touched them did not leave a stale dump, it left a partial
one -- a mysqldump killed midway, a snapshot half written -- and fetching that
would carry a corrupt copy home and call it the state of the guest.  So that one
dies, before anything is fetched.

=cut

# The guest a salvage talks to: the one about to be rebuilt, reached as the admin
# user with the key this run has already written into the config directory.
#
sub _salvage_guest {
    my ( $static_ip, $admin_user, $cfg_dir ) = @_;

    # The address may be a cidr, which is not a thing to connect to.
    ( my $host = $static_ip ) =~ s{/.*\z}{};

    # One connection per guest, and not one per recipe.  Both callers ask for
    # this inside the loop over the modules -- refresh_salvage once a module and
    # the fetch once for each thing that module salvages -- and leaving the
    # keeping of it to them means an ssh handshake apiece against a machine
    # that is about to be thrown away.
    #
    # Keyed on everything that decides which machine this is and how it is
    # reached, because one run configures several domains and each is a
    # different guest.  Process wide rather than hung on an object, since there
    # is no object here to hang it on.
    state %guest;
    my $id = join( "\0", $host, $admin_user // q{}, $cfg_dir // q{} );
    return $guest{$id} if $guest{$id};

    # The key may not be here yet, in which case say nothing about one rather
    # than name a file that is not there: ssh has an agent and a config of its
    # own, and either is a better answer than an -i nothing can read.
    ## no critic (ValuesAndExpressions::ProhibitFiletest_f)
    my $key = "$cfg_dir/key.rsa";
    undef $key unless -f $key;

    return $guest{$id} = Trog::Guest->new(
        name => $host,
        host => $host,
        user => $admin_user,
        ( $key ? ( key_path => $key ) : () ),
    );
}

sub refresh_salvage {
    my ( $static_ip, $admin_user, $cfg_dir, $module, $commands, $watched ) = @_;

    return unless @$commands;
    $watched //= [];

    my $guest = _salvage_guest( $static_ip, $admin_user, $cfg_dir );
    my $host  = $guest->name;

    my %seen = _salvage_mtimes( $guest, $watched );

    foreach my $command (@$commands) {
        print "Asking $host to run '$command' before salvaging $module\n";

        # Through a shell, because what a recipe hands over is a command line
        # rather than an argv -- a script with its arguments, or a pipeline.
        my $failed = $guest->run_sudo( '/bin/sh', '-c', $command );

        # Taken again whether it worked or not: a command that succeeded is
        # supposed to have changed things, and comparing the next one against
        # what was there before this one ran would blame it for that.
        my %now     = _salvage_mtimes( $guest, $watched );
        my @touched = grep { ( $seen{$_} // q{} ) ne ( $now{$_} // q{} ) } sort keys %now;
        %seen = %now;

        next unless $failed;

        die "$module: '$command' failed on $host after changing what it was refreshing:\n" . join( q{}, map { "    $_\n" } @touched ) . "That is a partial dump rather than a stale one, and fetching it would carry\n" . "a corrupt copy home as though it were the state of the guest.  Nothing was\n" . "fetched.  Fix the guest, or move what it left aside, and run this again.\n"
          if @touched;

        warn "$module: '$command' failed on $host, so what comes down is whatever was already there.\n" . "Whatever it was going to refresh is as old as the last time something did.\n";
    }

    return 1;
}

# The newest mtime anywhere under each path, one number apiece.  The mtime of a
# directory only moves when an entry is added or removed, so asking the
# directories alone would miss a dump rewritten in place, which is the usual
# case being watched for.
#
# Absent paths, and ones the probe cannot read, come back empty and compare
# equal to each other, so a path that did not exist before or after is not a
# change.
sub _salvage_mtimes {
    my ( $guest, $watched ) = @_;
    return () unless @$watched;

    my $script = join "\n", map {
        ( my $q = $_ ) =~ s/'/'\\''/g;
        "printf '%s\\t%s\\n' '$q' \"\$(find '$q' -printf '%T@\\n' 2>/dev/null | sort -rn | head -1)\""
    } @$watched;

    ( my $quoted = $script ) =~ s/'/'\\''/g;
    my $said = $guest->capture_cmd("sudo sh -c '$quoted'") // q{};

    my %mtime;
    foreach my $line ( split "\n", $said ) {
        my ( $path, $stamp ) = split "\t", $line, 2;
        next unless defined $path && length $path;
        $mtime{$path} = defined $stamp ? $stamp : q{};
    }

    return %mtime;
}

=head2 write_guest_secrets($cfg_dir, $fqdn, $wanted, $store, $keepass)

Make sure the secret store holds a value for everything the recipes asked to
have placed on the guest, and write down where each one goes.

The manifest carries the reference, never the value.  It sits beside the domain
rather than in the payload precisely so that these do not go the way
C<remote_files> state goes -- into the data directory, into the tarball, and into
every backup taken of it.  C<bin/provision> reads it, resolves the references,
and puts the files on the guest before the makefile runs.

=cut

sub write_guest_secrets {
    my ( $cfg_dir, $fqdn, $wanted, $store, $keepass ) = @_;

    my $manifest = "$cfg_dir/guest-secrets.yaml";
    unlink $manifest;
    return unless %$wanted;

    # Asking for the password here rather than at the top: a run whose recipes
    # want nothing placed should not be made to produce one.
    $keepass //= Trog::Credentials->prompt( 'Enter password:', 'keepass' );

    my %generator = map { $wanted->{$_}{ref} => $wanted->{$_}{generate} } keys %$wanted;
    my %held      = Trog::Secrets->remember( $store, $keepass, %generator );

    my %manifest = map {
        $_ => {
            ref   => $wanted->{$_}{ref},
            owner => $wanted->{$_}{owner} // 'root:root',
            mode  => $wanted->{$_}{mode}  // '0600',
        }
    } keys %$wanted;

    File::Slurper::Temp::write_binary( $manifest, YAML::XS::Dump( \%manifest ) );
    chmod 0600, $manifest;

    print "Wrote $manifest: " . scalar( keys %manifest ) . " file(s) for bin/provision to place on $fqdn\n";
    print "  $_ <- $manifest{$_}{ref}\n" for sort keys %manifest;

    return \%held;
}

# Copy the helper scripts into the domain's directory so they get tarred up with
# everything else.
#
# Modes come along deliberately: every one of these is executed on the guest, and
# File::Copy does not promise to preserve the executable bit.
sub pack_scripts {
    my ( $checkout, $cfg_dir ) = @_;

    my $from = "$checkout/scripts";
    die "No scripts directory at $from\n" unless -d $from;

    my $to = "$cfg_dir/scripts";
    remove_tree($to) if -d $to;
    make_path($to) or die "Could not create $to: $!\n";

    my @packed;
    foreach my $script ( Provisioner::Utils::files_in($from) ) {

        # Opening it proves we may read it; files_in has already left out the
        # directories.
        open( my $probe, '<', "$from/$script" ) or next;
        close $probe;
        copy( "$from/$script", "$to/$script" )
          or die "Could not copy $script into $to: $!\n";
        ## no critic (Plicease::ProhibitLeadingZeros) -- the policy exempts a
        ## literal handed straight to chmod; this is a mask against a mode.
        chmod( ( stat("$from/$script") )[2] & 07777, "$to/$script" );
        push @packed, "scripts/$script";
    }

    print "Packed " . scalar(@packed) . " scripts from $from\n";
    return @packed;
}

# Say whether a salvage that landed nothing is a problem, and what kind.
#
# Ask the destination rather than the transfer.  A transfer reports what it moved
# on this pass, and a second run against an unchanged guest legitimately moves
# nothing, having taken the lot the first time -- so what moved says nothing
# about whether the salvage has anything in it.
#
# An empty destination is one of three things, and only the guest can tell them
# apart.  The path may not be there at all, which is what every first build of a
# recipe looks like.  It may be there and hold nothing, which is a service that
# has started and not yet written anything.  Or it may hold files that did not
# come down, which is the one worth stopping a rebuild over.
#
# Asked of the guest as root, for the same reason the fetch is: the paths a
# recipe salvages belong to the service and nobody else can open them, so an
# unprivileged look inside one cannot tell "empty" from "not mine to read" and
# would call every one of them alarming.
#
# It used to be two things rather than three, because the fetch was
# unprivileged and unreadable was the likely case -- so a directory that was
# genuinely empty got a warning blaming permissions, and a directory that was
# unreadable was the thing the warning was actually for.  Now that the fetch can
# read them, the remaining alarming case is a fetch that failed.
sub _salvage_gap {
    my (%args) = @_;

    return () if _dir_has_files( $args{destination} );

    # The path as an argument rather than interpolated into the command: this is
    # a shell, and run_sudo escapes each element for us.
    #
    # find -print -quit stops at the first file rather than walking a state
    # directory that may be very large, and says nothing for a path that is not
    # there -- so one question covers both of the quiet cases, a path that was
    # never created and one the service has not written into.
    my $rc = $args{guest}->run_sudo(
        'sh',          '-c', 'test -n "$(find "$1" -type f -print -quit 2>/dev/null)"',
        'salvage-gap', $args{remote},
    );

    my %gap = ( recipe => $args{recipe}, remote => $args{remote} );

    # test exits 0 or 1 and nothing else, so anything else is the question not
    # having been asked -- a guest that went away mid-run, or a sudo that was
    # refused.  Filing that as a service which has never run would put the alarm
    # out on state that is still there, which is the failure this whole sub
    # exists to prevent.
    return {
        %gap,
        alarming => 1,
        message  => "$args{recipe}: could not ask $args{host} whether $args{remote} still holds anything" . " -- the check exited $rc as $args{user}.  Treating that as state that did not come off," . " because the alternative is calling a guest we cannot reach a guest with nothing on it.",
    } if $rc > 1;

    return {
        %gap,
        alarming => 0,
        message  => "$args{recipe}: nothing under $args{remote} on $args{host} yet, so there is nothing to salvage.",
    } if $rc;

    return {
        %gap,
        alarming => 1,
        message  => "$args{recipe}: salvaged nothing out of $args{remote} on $args{host}, and $args{destination} is empty." . "  The guest still has files there, so this is a fetch that failed rather than a service with no state." . "  Rebuilding $args{host} now loses them.",
    };
}

# Whether a salvage landed anything, anywhere under its destination.
#
# Files rather than entries: rget makes the local directories on the way down
# whether or not it can read what is inside them, so a tree of empty directories
# is precisely what an unreadable fetch leaves behind.
#
# It stops at the first file it finds instead of walking the tree, because one
# file answers the whole question and some of what gets salvaged is very large.
# Symlinks count as a file and are not followed: one salvaged off a guest can
# point anywhere, including back up the tree it is in.
sub _dir_has_files {
    my ($dir) = @_;
    return 0 unless -d $dir;

    # Stopped at the first one rather than counted: whether there is anything at
    # all is the whole question, and some of what gets salvaged is very large.
    # die is how you leave File::Find early, so the find is wrapped and the
    # message it throws is the answer.
    # A reference rather than a string, compared by identity below.  File::Find
    # appends its own context to a death on the way out of wanted, so a message
    # does not arrive as it was thrown and cannot be told from a real error.
    my $found = \'found';

    my $ok = eval {
        File::Find::find(
            {
                no_chdir => 1,
                wanted   => sub {

                    # Symlinks count and are not followed: one salvaged off a
                    # guest can point anywhere, including back up its own tree.
                    ## no critic (ValuesAndExpressions::ProhibitFiletest_l, ValuesAndExpressions::ProhibitFiletest_d)
                    die $found if -l $File::Find::name || !-d $File::Find::name;
                },
            },
            $dir
        );
        1;
    };
    return 0 if $ok;

    my $err = $@;
    return 1 if ref $err && ref $err eq 'SCALAR' && $err == $found;
    die $err;
}

sub _get_sftp {
    my ( $static_ip, $admin_user, $cfg_dir ) = @_;

    # IP might be cidr, we don't care about that
    $static_ip =~ s|/.*$||;
    my $target_is_up = 0;

    my $key2use = "$cfg_dir/key.rsa";

    # Key may not be here yet
    ## no critic (ValuesAndExpressions::ProhibitFiletest_f)
    $key2use = undef unless -f $key2use;

    my $ssh = Net::SFTP::Foreign->new(
        host     => $static_ip,
        user     => $admin_user,
        more     => [ -o => "StrictHostKeyChecking=no", -o => 'UserKnownHostsFile=/dev/null' ],
        key_path => $key2use
    );
    $ssh->error or $target_is_up = 1;
    return ( $ssh, $target_is_up );
}

sub get_dns_provider_token {
    my ($rconf) = @_;
    my @known_dns_providers = qw{pdns};
    foreach my $provider (@known_dns_providers) {
        return $rconf->{$provider}{api_key} if exists $rconf->{$provider} && ( ref( $rconf->{$provider} ) eq 'HASH' );
    }
    return '';
}

main(@ARGV) unless caller;
